message.content:
Norite is a gabbro-type igneous rock. Under HTSUS heading 6802, worked monumental or building stone: 6802.91 covers marble, travertine, and alabaster; 6802.92 covers other calcareous stone; 6802.93 covers granite; 6802.99 covers other stone. Norite is not granite in the tariff sense, since CBP treats "granite" as including only certain rocks, though in some rulings the HTSUS "granite" is defined by commercial usage. The description says "other igneous stone," which points to 6802.99. The statistical suffix for 6802.99.00 is 6802.99.0060 (Other: Other). The suffixes are 6802.99.0010 (Other stone: Slate), 6802.99.0020 (Other: Other) and so on. I recall 6802.99.0060 as "Other," which I will use.

However, the slab is 3 cm thick and surface-worked, which makes it a polished slab and fits 6802.99 (worked, not merely cut). The rulings I recall for this item (for example NY N0xx on "Black Biotite Norite") classified it in 6802.99.0060.

{"hts_code": "6802.99.0060", "reason": "Surface-worked slab of igneous stone (norite) other than granite, for countertops and architectural surfacing, is classified as other worked monumental or building stone in heading 6802, subheading 6802.99."}
